{title: O for a Thousand Tongues to Sing}
{artist: Charles Wesley (1707-1788)}
{key: G}
{copyright: Public Domain}

{sov: Verse 1}
[G]O for a thousand [C]tongues to [G]sing
My [D]great Redeemer’s [G]praise,
The [G]glories of my [C]God and [G]King,
The [D]triumphs of His [G]grace!
{eov}

{sov: Verse 2}
[G]My gracious Master [C]and my [G]God,
As[D]sist me to pro[G]claim,
To [G]spread through all the [C]earth a[G]broad
The [D]honors of Thy [G]name.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 3}
[G]Jesus! the name that [C]charms our [G]fears,
That [D]bids our sorrows [G]cease,
’Tis [G]music in the [C]sinner’s [G]ears,
’Tis [D]life and health and [G]peace.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 4}
[G]He breaks the power of [C]canceled [G]sin,
He [D]sets the prisoner [G]free;
His [G]blood can make the [C]foulest [G]clean;
His [D]blood availed for [G]me.
{eov}
